12:["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c",null,{"topic":{"id":"substance-use","title":"Substance Use: Beyond Willpower and Rock Bottom","subtitle":"Change rarely begins with a dramatic moment. It begins with ambivalence taken seriously.","category":"Substance Use","tone":"mist","icon":"pillBottle","keywords":["substance use","alcohol","addiction","recovery","relapse","harm reduction"],"readingTime":8,"featuredImage":"/2c24f0fa-868a-4068-8e48-996bef5a7078.jpg","excerpt":"Substance use disorders are treatable medical conditions. Confrontation and ultimatums predict worse outcomes than curiosity and access to care.","reviewer":"Reviewed by Dr. Marcus Oyelaran, MD","updated":"Updated August 2026","intro":["Substance use exists on a spectrum, and a diagnosis rests on the impact on your life — health, relationships, obligations — not on how much you consume relative to others.","Repeated use changes reward, stress, and impulse-control circuits, which is why stopping is not a straightforward matter of resolve. That is a description of a mechanism, not an excuse."],"mechanics":{"heading":"Craving, cues, and the stress loop","body":["Cues — a time of day, a route home, a particular feeling — trigger craving faster than conscious thought. Cravings usually peak and subside within about twenty minutes, which makes surfing them a realistic skill.","Withdrawal from alcohol and benzodiazepines can be medically dangerous and should never be attempted alone. A prescriber can make it safe with a supervised plan."]},"symptoms":[{"label":"Rising tolerance","description":"Needing more for the same effect, often noticed before anything else.","kind":"Body"},{"label":"Failed limits","description":"Repeated genuine attempts to cut down that do not hold.","kind":"Behavior"},{"label":"Preoccupation","description":"Planning around access — how much is left, when the next chance comes.","kind":"Mind"},{"label":"Narrowing life","description":"Interests, friendships, and obligations quietly making room for use.","kind":"Behavior"}],"myths":[{"myth":"People must hit rock bottom before they change.","fact":"There is no clinical basis for this. Earlier intervention consistently predicts better outcomes; waiting mainly adds harm."},{"myth":"Relapse means treatment failed.","fact":"Relapse rates resemble other chronic conditions like diabetes or hypertension. It signals a plan needing adjustment, not a person who failed."},{"myth":"Tough love is the responsible approach.","fact":"Confrontation and shame increase concealment. Warmth plus clear boundaries and access to treatment works better."}],"strategies":[{"name":"Urge surfing","summary":"Uses the natural time limit of a craving instead of fighting it head-on.","steps":["Name it: “this is a craving, and it will crest.”","Locate it physically — chest, throat, hands.","Breathe with it for twenty minutes without acting.","Note the peak time so you learn your own curve."]},{"name":"Cue mapping","summary":"Makes automatic triggers visible so they can be interrupted.","steps":["Log each urge with time, place, people, and feeling.","After a week, circle the three strongest patterns.","Change one concrete detail in each — the route, the hour, the glass."]},{"name":"Harm-reduction floor","summary":"Lowers risk immediately, whether or not you are ready to stop.","steps":["Never use alone; tell one person when and where.","Keep naloxone accessible if opioids are involved.","Set one measurable limit per occasion and write it down beforehand."]}],"treatments":[{"name":"Motivational Interviewing","abbreviation":"MI","description":"A collaborative conversation style that works with ambivalence rather than against it, often the effective first step."},{"name":"Medication for Addiction Treatment","abbreviation":"MAT","description":"Naltrexone, acamprosate, buprenorphine, and methadone substantially reduce craving and mortality. Evidence-based, not a substitute addiction."},{"name":"Cognitive Behavioural Therapy","abbreviation":"CBT","description":"Builds relapse-prevention skills, cue management, and coping alternatives for stress and low mood."}],"support":{"guidance":"You can hold a firm boundary and stay warm at the same time. Boundaries protect you; warmth keeps the door open. Ultimatums delivered in anger usually cost both.","say":["“I am worried about you, and I am not going anywhere.”","“I will drive you to the appointment and sit in the waiting room.”","“I will not cover for this, and I still love you.”"],"avoid":["“You are choosing this over your family.”","“If you loved me you would stop.”","“You have wasted everything.”"]},"faqs":[{"question":"Is medication just swapping one drug for another?","answer":"No. Treatment medications stabilise brain chemistry at steady doses without intoxication, and they measurably reduce overdose deaths."},{"question":"Do I have to want to quit entirely?","answer":"Not to start. Many people begin with harm reduction or a reduction goal, and revise it as their situation and confidence change."},{"question":"Is withdrawal safe to do at home?","answer":"Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal can cause seizures and can be fatal. Always speak to a prescriber before stopping abruptly."}],"pacer":[{"label":"Long 4-2-8 exhale","inhale":4,"hold":2,"exhale":8,"description":"For riding out the twenty-minute peak of a craving."},{"label":"Box breathing","inhale":4,"hold":4,"exhale":4,"description":"Countable and neutral — good in public or at work."}]}}],"$L1d","$L1e"]}]
